    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5306",
      "problem": "Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x} - 2.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242844/assets/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nSince we have $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}{\\color{red}\\,-\\,2},$ we need to translate the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ *down* by $\\color{red}2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y=\\dfrac 1 x$ is translated down by $2$ units, the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ is also translated down by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new horizontal asymptote is $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242844/assets/b157913f6fca24190330cdfa5c795e069eff15de8a2079d9e29e1d6e41b95cb4.png)",
